<h1>Notes</h1>

<p class="lede">Release notes and short writing from the development of FlatSixDynamics. We post when there is something worth posting.</p>

<article class="release">
    <h2>Version 1.6</h2>
    <p class="release-date">July 2026</p>
    <p>Version 1.6 introduces the Track Wing Lab &mdash; and gives every flow study a path to follow.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>New &mdash; Track Wing Lab:</strong> push a wing's angle past its working range and watch the flow start to buffet, live &mdash; not a number on a chart, the thing itself.</li>
        <li><strong>Tracer pathlines, everywhere:</strong> the Cylinder, Airfoil, and Track Wing studies now trace continuous paths through the air, alongside the existing color field.</li>
        <li><strong>A plain-language read on the narrowing-channel case:</strong> a short line under the slider explains what's happening as you push the flow harder &mdash; no jargon required.</li>
        <li><strong>A tighter Lab layout:</strong> panels, toggles, and readouts refreshed for clarity across every scene.</li>
        <li><strong>Smoother throughout,</strong> with refined visuals and a handful of stability fixes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Thank you for flying with us.</p>
</article>

<article class="release">
    <h2>Version 1.4</h2>
    <p class="release-date">July 2026</p>
    <p>Version 1.4 introduces the Setup Lab &mdash; ride height, live, and no ranking attached.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>New &mdash; Setup Lab:</strong> step through three ride-height stages, Serie, Sport, and Track, and watch the body settle visibly lower over the underbody flow.</li>
        <li><strong>Setup delta, honestly:</strong> a readout shows exactly what each stage changes &mdash; plain numbers, no ranking, no claim of a &ldquo;best&rdquo; setup.</li>
        <li><strong>New help sheet:</strong> a walkthrough of how the splitter lip and the diffuser trade off against each other, and how that balance shifts with speed.</li>
        <li><strong>A leaner control panel:</strong> more compact throughout, so the underbody view has more room to breathe.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Thank you for flying with us.</p>
</article>

<article class="release">
    <h2>Version 1.2</h2>
    <p class="release-date">June 21, 2026</p>
    <p>Version 1.2 introduces live streamlines &mdash; and makes the whole app faster.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>New &mdash; Streamlines:</strong> watch the airflow trace continuous lines over the car in real time, alongside the pressure and vector views.</li>
        <li><strong>Faster throughout:</strong> a leaner graphics path delivers a quicker launch and a smoother, more responsive live simulation on device.</li>
        <li><strong>A cleaner flow field:</strong> the wake behind the car and the underbody now read more continuously.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Thank you for flying with us.</p>
</article>

<article class="release">
    <h2>Version 1.1</h2>
    <p class="release-date">June 2026</p>
    <p>Version 1.1 opens up the Labs.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Flow Labs for everyone:</strong> the three interactive flow studies are no longer hidden &mdash; the cylinder wake, the airfoil, and the nozzle, whenever you like.</li>
        <li><strong>Velocity-colored vectors:</strong> flow arrows take their color from local speed, so acceleration and separation read at a glance.</li>
        <li><strong>Deeper explanations:</strong> an expanded introduction and in-app Deep Dives walk you through the physics behind each scene.</li>
        <li><strong>Pinch to zoom:</strong> move in close on the boundary layer, the shock, the wake.</li>
        <li><strong>Smoother and faster:</strong> a reworked render path keeps the simulation fluid on a wider range of devices, with a faster, more reliable launch.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Thank you for flying with us.</p>
</article>
